#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063912 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/id #uuid "121d5f8c-3b38-5c75-aec1-646dc7d2f2f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{4+5x}$ and the point $P\\left(-1,-5\\right)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y=f^{-1}(x)$ at the point $\\left(-5,-1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(-1, -5).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2-3x}{4+5x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{22}{(4+5x)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-22\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(-1, -5)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(-5, -1)$ lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ \n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-22)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{22}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063913 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "d113528f-a3ff-5f28-b071-bcf355a36b45" :question/math-academy-id "e-14720" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B \\,(0,2)$ lies on $f^{-1},$ we have $f^{-1}(0) = 2.$ We're also told that $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/id #uuid "9e531872-846f-5e75-97c0-dd2db3515558" :question/math-academy-id "e-14721" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x), f'(x),g(x),$ and $g'(x)$ at some points. Find $(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | $f'(x)$ | $g(x)$ | $g'(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1})'(x) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $f^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(x)=\\dfrac{g(x)}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}+g'(x)f^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n(f^{-1}\\cdot g)'(1) =\\dfrac{g(1)}{f'(f^{-1}(1))}+g'(1)f^{-1}(1).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain $f^{-1}(1)=2,$ $g(1)=-3,$ $g'(1)=1,$ and $f'(2)=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(1) & =\\frac{g(1)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}+{g}^{′}(1){f}^{-1}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(2)}+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-1}+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "d38ac709-38e8-51a1-8e08-14913c77639c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14722" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x) =3e^{x}-1.$ Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})'(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $(f^{-1})'(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(2) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of $f^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation $f^{-1}(2) = x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-1 & =2 \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & =3 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f^{-1}(2) = 0.$\n\nNow, we calculate $f'(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{e}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c92cfc0c-0b9d-5ea7-b010-72abd6d1d49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388150} {:answer/id #uuid "157fc464-b501-5e51-9aa0-735d660c5d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388151} {:answer/id #uuid "f48bbaab-5ab6-5c21-baf4-cca447254bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388152} {:answer/id #uuid "b908e1e5-7546-541e-8b71-08859b50e757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388153} {:answer/id #uuid "e98de654-05d8-50a7-b097-e31f2fd0c5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b908e1e5-7546-541e-8b71-08859b50e757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388153} :answer-field/id #uuid "64bafd13-4937-5c24-b2ed-715d4d5a8781" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "728516cc-a318-5bc6-87a9-bed32f2e0ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-110307" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$  of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $-3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=-3,$ $g(4)=3,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{3}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32283e5a-58c1-5838-ae16-131a056cae36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388157} {:answer/id #uuid "0443ee2b-27c2-5660-b22f-c7e876d28e6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388158} {:answer/id #uuid "27b756d2-b835-573f-844a-24dd03d31203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388159} {:answer/id #uuid "b7575923-a50b-5387-8c92-29289d6ae0e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388160} {:answer/id #uuid "270648f4-f099-56ad-a495-b9645d40f66a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27b756d2-b835-573f-844a-24dd03d31203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388159} :answer-field/id #uuid "efdfbf35-7dd9-5182-8244-71da7be1b9b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f7f5d03-a853-5e3f-88b3-f143c3384e25" :question/math-academy-id "q-110308" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3)={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & ={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9773492-dd72-5047-9ee5-cb02d71d9e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388164} {:answer/id #uuid "eacd902e-01d0-5056-8826-5245394852c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388165} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d77215-4d73-53d9-bb8d-66dbeec46e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388166} {:answer/id #uuid "345f13cb-9c41-55bd-b227-0e7bd5ad4c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388167} {:answer/id #uuid "b06a95dc-1400-50f5-8af4-7eda09d1a504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d77215-4d73-53d9-bb8d-66dbeec46e0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388166} :answer-field/id #uuid "32082d83-d392-5ea3-8624-0af973a39806" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab71d05c-b918-52c2-bf47-2e847a369a63" :question/math-academy-id "q-110345" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}-7.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(9).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(9)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(9) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x}-7 & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{4} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(9)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}-7 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =16\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "480b4459-f881-5842-b5e9-cea3977473e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388171} {:answer/id #uuid "26a44af3-d49f-5e88-a79a-779e5caf3e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388172} {:answer/id #uuid "ff84c467-f387-5c94-9dd6-43c96bf71358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388173} {:answer/id #uuid "471cad84-e584-5ae6-9ac1-de074e965d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388174} {:answer/id #uuid "a61fce5a-e8b2-551e-be81-c08266127b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a61fce5a-e8b2-551e-be81-c08266127b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388175} :answer-field/id #uuid "37c6be68-8840-526d-bb62-7a0a60f78f74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cd6d2ebf-c817-5eeb-8275-41ef01d7d0f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110497" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={3}^{x}-1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x}-1 & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b6f916d-f3e3-512b-a028-02b013492925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061295} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6fdd01-fed9-5174-8011-bdec233c5ef5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061296} {:answer/id #uuid "e57cb073-71da-58d6-a9a7-bf9ce66c66e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061297} {:answer/id #uuid "0d2917cb-81d1-5307-acb1-1f7f0c085c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061298} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe95abb-6ad4-56f0-8716-fd0a07503731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fe95abb-6ad4-56f0-8716-fd0a07503731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061299} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d9afe78-8e13-5789-ac61-14e82575ffe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e3de030-cb78-5dd0-88f5-7229520c5041" :question/math-academy-id "q-15667" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/24d83be9fed627018111aff41b976881c98c6c58b2ceeb0ff11c033f9926afd3.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82b8972-b95e-55ce-bcc9-8e341f638474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388178} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccbf55f-9a07-545f-a8ba-d2c3ad4391d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388179} {:answer/id #uuid "1a009ec3-38b2-58d3-bd39-07cdbcb9fc57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388180} {:answer/id #uuid "adfe614d-1072-5ba8-bb11-89111e082860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388181} {:answer/id #uuid "15ed495a-c04d-583c-a31b-0c8a110f169c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ccbf55f-9a07-545f-a8ba-d2c3ad4391d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388179} :answer-field/id #uuid "f522d3ce-6ba8-5da3-a044-ae7dcac7962c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f26d29b2-a2a2-52d8-94c1-570c7b1f917d" :question/math-academy-id "q-15716" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/a7a711eed2b4805f0e9dfc64c0992acfef26430e37f8df446b08055143a6cadc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,5)$ and lies on $y=f.$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(5,2)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(2)=4,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(5,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(2)=4.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2224d98b-c515-5ca1-8172-2c517793da4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388185} {:answer/id #uuid "176a76e3-386b-51ea-be11-acdb252362af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388186} {:answer/id #uuid "2246cda6-4be2-516f-bcb4-ca244d23ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388187} {:answer/id #uuid "ff8b1edd-a11b-5cd3-a263-27bbffb1d39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388188} {:answer/id #uuid "82c76660-31ae-5aff-b80d-41e76740bcdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2246cda6-4be2-516f-bcb4-ca244d23ba68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388187} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7ec39b-c724-5e50-93d1-f69e7fa49ca8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01aa62d5-9416-5775-b66b-204e3e2095b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-15719" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/517276d5122f1a81b894da7be7796c5941a16442987f99f476220979bc5688c7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2.6)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(2.6,3)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2.6)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(2.6,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2.6)=3.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2.6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2.6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5110235-92c1-5a27-a99c-d71e2fe24850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061398} {:answer/id #uuid "ce568ea6-ae0b-5a67-87a9-94b73170df32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061399} {:answer/id #uuid "da1b6afb-a864-5662-b19c-7fa8166bccb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061400} {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} {:answer/id #uuid "7c66de25-cfef-5e04-98e5-09487adc4a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3f4f87-07a3-561b-a1e5-5bea819e76c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34e33e76-2a83-5368-8561-def1a916040d" :question/math-academy-id "q-19201" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)=\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1,$ $g(2)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2) & =\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d59c5fa-d16d-5964-be9a-d24160ac2459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061721} {:answer/id #uuid "a27c6cb9-ef07-5ca8-9003-3fff68b1026f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061722} {:answer/id #uuid "f18bd396-41cf-5638-bf20-b68401a7d198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061723} {:answer/id #uuid "2f1d80e0-2855-553b-88c5-29018aa2e456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061724} {:answer/id #uuid "4299c6dd-fe94-5bce-a2c5-9538d740d7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4299c6dd-fe94-5bce-a2c5-9538d740d7c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061725} :answer-field/id #uuid "6541b2f6-8aee-5eaf-9d8c-9e8524f4879b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e89d0fd-c938-54a1-9b82-cef992b23366" :question/math-academy-id "q-2294" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3$ and the point $P(2,11)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,11).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,11)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(11,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(11))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac85305-fe64-5710-a7c0-b59a5a1fac45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388192} {:answer/id #uuid "c01b9e4d-9150-583f-9000-e05e952acf6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388193} {:answer/id #uuid "e44184de-708c-556e-b483-1c70619b588d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388194} {:answer/id #uuid "7224e289-6af6-598b-acd8-8752ff81d921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388195} {:answer/id #uuid "057a795f-6f79-5b8c-8096-4ddee3f29aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c01b9e4d-9150-583f-9000-e05e952acf6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388193} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a1d767c-4ca6-570e-8141-da96224c934b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388191}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "469ade2f-f4b3-58d6-a683-fc6ee5ddd73f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2526" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} {:answer/id #uuid "0f65b70c-4513-5d7f-88de-bf7e421f1942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295810} :answer-field/id #uuid "40350093-241a-55dd-ac9f-9a83f78e707e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c43205-3a68-5865-9baf-3488c8c39bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-312578" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5595ee00-3e08-5c85-ab82-a1dbb68af433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} {:answer/id #uuid "83ac79de-d929-59e9-b4cd-f03e4d332524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5595ee00-3e08-5c85-ab82-a1dbb68af433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} :answer-field/id #uuid "822f713b-c25e-5da8-a950-9db41fcefb5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1f6eff9-e354-50e5-b0d6-7aed9742e28f" :question/math-academy-id "q-312579" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and the point $P(2,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295816 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295818} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd9d412-937f-5c3d-b8be-8ecf613b4921" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295817}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1490c31-6761-5f3f-8b46-c37dbd062af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-312583" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295824} {:answer/id #uuid "9279453a-70a9-5b54-8d67-c3311cf7e8b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295824} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e16358-519e-5455-b910-148e3f8eb26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "422cbd5e-38e5-544a-b056-53061bf5421a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342562" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f2a9871-30c7-5f85-8f7c-27bc28d08d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f2a9871-30c7-5f85-8f7c-27bc28d08d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388200} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90ef847-b4bf-5479-a810-e9c0d1177d9d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6cd4a653-e15a-56e6-a54e-5c8af74b1421" :question/math-academy-id "q-342581" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,4)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295834} {:answer/id #uuid "0998d20c-9922-58be-aec2-a73bf65cba6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295834} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee9e45-6561-505c-b53a-1631b71fe964" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "693dd3d0-3454-5894-a36e-0df2728d3681" :question/math-academy-id "q-342583" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c011dca-d08a-55e4-a0ce-0ee30927aa38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388204} {:answer/id #uuid "3c58aabf-fc98-57de-8141-7ea37cba24d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c011dca-d08a-55e4-a0ce-0ee30927aa38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388204} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d1ac83-e100-52eb-aaef-8007d5f0ddcf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41d98065-acca-5eb2-82b3-4c1ae5f6a004" :question/math-academy-id "q-342597" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0)={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=-3,$ $g(0)=1,$ ${g}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & ={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93df8dc9-5514-5610-9ba7-f8141c1ad15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063694} {:answer/id #uuid "aa054826-9bc5-55fa-91f8-c8a1331439de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063695} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9e50e9-5afc-5ec8-9608-e1478bb62e6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063696} {:answer/id #uuid "02eb72f8-e800-5b85-8c72-f9a7b308c956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063697} {:answer/id #uuid "19c1d878-07a5-503d-8555-81b1fc1b9466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19c1d878-07a5-503d-8555-81b1fc1b9466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063698} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4358ed-8a57-5c21-93f2-ea7a5edec4a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3dd725b3-7f44-5fca-9ff0-b43869ecbad5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75986" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4).$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(0).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(0)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(0) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=0$ since the logarithm is not defined here. Therefore, ${f}^{-1}(0)=5.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(5),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4) \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-4)+\\frac{x}{x-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4)+\\frac{5}{5-4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106944ca-a68d-5131-99a4-0e2271631df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388208} {:answer/id #uuid "22a78c11-6322-5d7a-94d8-28662b7d9bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388209} {:answer/id #uuid "f50fc084-f9c3-55ce-be8a-d3f4aaa6d598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388210} {:answer/id #uuid "4ebcd5f2-d502-5f14-b4c8-58bda3316f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388211} {:answer/id #uuid "8139a2ba-0431-5b1f-bfb8-efbdacc2e356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ebcd5f2-d502-5f14-b4c8-58bda3316f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388211} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcee70ef-3e60-5eb7-8716-131a1e19306d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f01d7ed-1d8a-5bb3-a9a6-4686958c8b4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-76115" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ 1 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2a80aa-7882-50eb-bfe0-c0eeab72161d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Derivative Using Properties of Inverse Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59821fc8-e9df-5397-8f51-eb936149f62d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{-4}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295855} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d66dab-93a0-5e57-b08d-c5fef1224017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295856} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e7b9cc-271b-5641-9a48-5cb756f4d2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295857} {:answer/id #uuid "3bea40ac-6a9a-5644-8b15-d06af405881c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295858} {:answer/id #uuid "35a03d42-f1c5-5c7f-ba9d-b0db949ac390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e7b9cc-271b-5641-9a48-5cb756f4d2e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295857} :answer-field/id #uuid "521de4c2-d1f7-5a30-9d43-b3d0a36305f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8be70d73-596d-5442-ba07-c1e2659c3b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76128" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=1+2{e}^{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(3).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(3)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(3) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =3 \\\\ 1+2{e}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x+1} & =2 \\\\ {e}^{x+1} & =1 \\\\ x+1 & =0 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(3)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =1+2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2{e}^{0} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
